= Benchmark contamination =

== Overview ==
'''Benchmark contamination''' occurs when benchmark evaluation items, answers, close derivatives, or benchmark-specific information influence a model's training or tuning so that the resulting benchmark score no longer cleanly measures independent generalization.

The problem is especially visible in large-scale pretrained models, but it applies more broadly to machine-learning evaluation.

== Direct contamination ==
Direct contamination occurs when benchmark examples or answers appear in training data.

This can happen accidentally when public benchmarks are included in large scraped corpora.

== Derivative and indirect contamination ==
Contamination can also arise through:

* paraphrases or near-duplicates;
* solution write-ups;
* benchmark-specific synthetic data;
* supervised fine-tuning on benchmark-derived examples;
* repeated prompt or system tuning against a known benchmark;
* evaluation-time adaptation that uses benchmark feedback.

Exact string overlap is therefore only one contamination mechanism.

== Effect on scores ==
Contamination can inflate benchmark performance by turning an intended test of generalization into partial recognition, memorization, or benchmark-specific adaptation.

The size of the effect depends on model, training regime, exposure frequency, similarity, and forgetting.

Contamination does not imply that every affected score is completely meaningless.

== Other benchmark failures ==
A benchmark can be poor without being contaminated.

Problems such as construct mismatch, annotation errors, narrow coverage, saturation, or metric gaming are separate validity threats.

